A||||6||batboy||speedometer ain't working
Z||000000||batboy||07-14-99||09:18 PM||||The speedo and temp gauge do not work in my 85. I swapped the speed sensor out with a known good one and swapped the main instrument cluster with a know good one, same story, gauges still don't work. The speedo has worked briefly for short periods of time in the past. So, it must be the wires (which were butchered when they did the engine swap) right? Or am I missing something? This is a carb engine with no computer. Should I just run new gauge wires or is there an easier way to fix this? I've spent a lot of trying to track this damn problem down.
Z||000001||lowCG||07-14-99||11:52 PM||||Just noticed my temp guage needle is pointing at the 12:00 position,can almost see it,yeah,neat huh? It worked when I drove the car home last week.Don't know how it is stuck there,maybe some of the nicotine I wiped from the interior ran down inside and goobered it up.<br>I would run a temporary jumper from the sender and see what happens.Just make sure the resistance circuitry for the guages is included in the circuit(should be inside the pod).
Z||000002||lowCG||07-15-99||04:04 AM||||oops,these cars don't use a voltage stabilizer for the guages.
Z||000003||Reckard||07-16-99||10:42 AM||||I had this problem with my speedo once and it ended up being the sensor wasnt grounded well enough to the trany.<p>
Z||000004||theogre||07-16-99||02:30 PM||||check all the slagging little ground leads on the tranny/block. There are a number of them in differant places. One of those that's wrecked could cause individual sensors to flip out. My 87's got around 10 grounds on the block. I keep getting an intermitant code, then other day I tightened some of those grounds that I found loose, now maybe the code is gone......... 
Z||000005||batboy||07-16-99||07:09 PM||||Ogre, one other time you suggested checking the ground wires to me. At that time, I did find one disconnected one and replaced another one I thought looked bad. However, the instruments still didn't work. It's possible another ground is missing entirely that I don't know about. My other Fiero has a 2.5 engine and you're right, ground wires up the wazoo. I might do as LowCG suggested, run a jumper wire (I got lots of wire). If that works, it must be in the harness. If not, maybe a missing ground wire like Orgre says. Any other ideas before I work on the beast this weekend again?
Z||000006||batboy||07-16-99||07:15 PM||||Reckard, How did you fix your speed sensor grounding problem? I did swap another good sensor in and that didn't work, but I remember seeing a lot of oil around that area. Should I remove the sensor again and clean and possibly wire brush where it plugs in?
